Subject: amd64/isel: jnz on a long only tests its low 32 bits
Origin: Filnix
Upstream-Status: Not submitted
Base: c0818978acec60ebb6167fade60fb7012cbf20ca

The IL document says that jnz compares only the 32 least significant
bits of a long argument to zero, and selcmp() does that when there is
no flag-setting instruction to reuse. But when the argument is defined
by a 64-bit add, sub, and, etc., seljmp() and selsel() reused its
flags, which reflect all 64 bits: with %x = 2^32, the code below took
@yes.

	%x =l add %a, %b
	jnz %x, @yes, @no

Compare the low word explicitly when the flags come from a long
instruction other than a comparison. A long and is no longer turned
into a test, since a word test would need its immediate truncated.
Regression: filnix nanofil/tests/qbe-bugs/jnz-long-flags.ssa.

diff --git a/amd64/isel.c b/amd64/isel.c
index 379244a..0f514de 100644
--- a/amd64/isel.c
+++ b/amd64/isel.c
@@ -558,6 +558,12 @@ selsel(Fn *fn, Blk *b, Ins *i, Num *tn)
 			*fi = (Ins){.op = Onop};
 		}
 	}
+	else if (fi->cls == Kl) {
+		/* only the low 32 bits are tested */
+		gencmp = 1;
+		cr[0] = r;
+		cr[1] = CON_Z;
+	}
 	else if (fi->op == Oand && t->nuse == 1
 	     && (rtype(fi->arg[0]) == RTmp ||
 	         rtype(fi->arg[1]) == RTmp)) {
@@ -632,6 +638,11 @@ seljmp(Blk *b, Fn *fn)
 		}
 		b->jmp.type = Jjf + c;
 	}
+	else if (fi->cls == Kl) {
+		/* only the low 32 bits are tested */
+		selcmp((Ref[2]){r, CON_Z}, Kw, 0, fn);
+		b->jmp.type = Jjf + Cine;
+	}
 	else if (fi->op == Oand && t->nuse == 1
 	     && (rtype(fi->arg[0]) == RTmp ||
 	         rtype(fi->arg[1]) == RTmp)) {
